        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 有钱",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "有钱 — yǒuqián — \"rich; well-off\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "他很有钱 tā hěn yǒuqián — He's very well off."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 钱 (qián) — ten strokes in two pieces: on the left the metal piece, five strokes; on the right two short bars, a long slanting hook, a stroke falling to the left across it, and a dot. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "有钱 (yǒuqián) is built from 有 'to have' and 钱 (qián) 'money', both inherited from Middle Chinese, hjuwX and dzjen (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription). The dictionary explains the second, after an older commentary, as money named after a spring, because it flows everywhere."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
